// src/v4/extdeps/formats/toml.dag
//
// Scope: TOML data format model — direction-agnostic.
// Anchor: https://toml.io/en/v1.0.0 (TOML v1.0.0 spec)
//
// Owns:
//   - TomlValue = String | Int | Float | Bool | Datetime | Array | InlineTable | Table (closed sum)
//   - Table = Map<NonEmptyStr, TomlValue>
//   - Datetime model (TOML §Date-Time: RFC 3339 subset)
//   - parse(String) -> Result<TomlValue, Diagnostic>
//   - emit(TomlValue) -> String
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/diagnostic.dag, std/collection.dag
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-4.6
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).

module v4.extdeps.formats.toml


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// CANONICAL-MIRROR (T-4.6 fan-out). EXACT-mirrors the ratified canonical
// src/v4/extdeps/formats/json.dag seam (PR #3184, T-4 mgr seam-review
// ratification msg_f5971a8a): the format's closed recursive value sum +
// node.dag A1 name-reference recursion + the Practice-4 five-pattern
// ledger; parse/emit carrier seam = `Outcome<TomlValue>` (the ONE
// operator-ratified D1 carrier, std/diagnostic.dag DECISIONS item **I**
// — NOT a per-file sum); operations deferred-with-named-owner. Same
// discipline, TOML-anchored facts.
//
// DECLARATIVE-NOW / OPERATIONS-DEFERRED-WITH-OWNER. Modeled NOW: the
// `TomlValue` data model. DEFERRED (🟡, named owner + dissolution
// trigger, NEVER improvised, NEVER a bodiless `fn` — the frozen v2 seed
// rejects bodiless `fn`, DECISIONS PARSE-1): the parse/emit bodies, the
// numeric interpretation of int/float lexemes, the temporal
// interpretation of the datetime lexeme, and the non-empty-key
// refinement. The operator-sanctioned pattern verified on
// std/collection.dag #3169 + canonical json.dag #3184.
//
// CARRIER SEAM = Outcome<TomlValue> (NOT a per-file sum). The header's
// `parse(String) -> Result<TomlValue, Diagnostic>` prose denotes the
// ratified D1 carrier `Outcome<T> = Produced { value: T } | Rejected
// { diagnostic: Diagnostic }` (std/diagnostic.dag, DECISIONS item **I**;
// `Result<…, Diagnostic>` prose names this same surface — no parallel
// `Result<ok, err>` in std/). When the parse op is authored (trigger
// below) it returns `Outcome<TomlValue>` — the SAME carrier all six
// T-4.6 formats consume.
//
// FAIL-CLOSED, NO FABRICATION (TOML v1.0.0; brief §C binding STOP).
// Every non-conforming input is `Rejected { diagnostic }` — never a
// partial parse, never a defaulted/guessed TomlValue. `Map` makes a
// duplicate key structurally unrepresentable; a parsed table is dup-free
// by construction — TOML 1.0 §Keys "Defining a key multiple times is
// invalid" makes duplicate-key INPUT a parse `Rejected` (no silent
// last-wins = no-engine).
//
// SCAFFOLD-HEADER vs SUBSTRATE — two tracked reconciliations (the
// systemic frozen-header-references-not-yet-existent-substrate pattern;
// sanctioned fix = kernel carrier now + 🟡 named-trigger deferral +
// SURFACE, never a silent header rewrite, never a STOP when json.dag's
// exact pattern covers it):
//   - `Table = Map<NonEmptyStr, TomlValue>` (immutable header) — v4 has
//     no `NonEmptyStr` (it is a String refinement; the refinement
//     substrate is not landed — std/text.dag is T-3 scaffold). The
//     carrier here is kernel `Map<String, TomlValue>`; the non-empty-key
//     refinement is deferred 🟡 (block below). NOTE (surfaced, not
//     silently encoded): TOML v1.0.0 §Keys permits an EMPTY QUOTED key
//     (`"" = "v"` is valid, though discouraged) — so a literal
//     `NonEmptyStr` key type would be STRICTER than TOML 1.0. The
//     eventual refined key type must be TOML-faithful (bare-key charset
//     / quoted-key rules), not a blanket non-empty; the parser enforces
//     §Keys fail-closed meanwhile. Flagged to T-4 mgr.
//   - `Datetime` — v4 has no temporal substrate; `TomlDatetime` carries
//     the verbatim RFC 3339 lexeme (the json.dag `JsonNumber`
//     lexeme-preserving decision applied to time), temporal
//     interpretation deferred 🟡.
//
// `Bool`/`String` are kernel-ambient (v2 seed; STRUCTURE.md "Kernel-
// ambient types"); `List<T>`/`Map<K,V>` are kernel container types
// (node.dag uses `List` unimported). That relaxes only the import edge —
// this file remains TOML's sole modeling authority (the std/logic.dag
// kernel-ambient discipline). No std/ import is needed: the data model
// uses only kernel-ambient carriers + self-recursion; the deferred
// carrier seam is named in prose, not structurally referenced.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TomlValue — the TOML v1.0.0 value: the closed, recursive set of the
// eight TOML value kinds. Anchor: TOML v1.0.0 — https://toml.io/en/v1.0.0
// (§String / §Integer / §Float / §Boolean / §"Offset Date-Time" …
// §"Local Time" / §Array / §"Inline Table" / §Table).
//
//   - TomlString    — §String (basic / multi-line / literal). Payload
//                      is the kernel `String` (escape / multi-line
//                      decoding is the deferred parse body's concern,
//                      not a data-model variant).
//   - TomlInt       — §Integer. Carries the VERBATIM source token
//                      (`lexeme`), NOT a numeric value — the json.dag
//                      `JsonNumber` lexeme-preserving decision: TOML
//                      separates the integer TOKEN (dec/hex/oct/bin,
//                      `_` separators) from its numeric value; the
//                      faithful integer carrier (std/integer.dag) is
//                      scaffold, so collapsing now fabricates a
//                      range/precision decision (no-fabrication) and
//                      breaks B-6 round-trip. Numeric interpretation
//                      deferred (🟡 below).
//   - TomlFloat     — §Float (incl. `inf`/`nan`). Verbatim `lexeme`
//                      for the same reason; faithful carrier
//                      std/float.dag is scaffold (IEEE-754 collapse =
//                      fabrication + breaks B-6).
//   - TomlBool      — §Boolean (`true`/`false`); payload the kernel
//                      `Bool` (one authority — std/logic.dag).
//   - TomlDatetime  — §"Offset Date-Time" / §"Local Date-Time" /
//                      §"Local Date" / §"Local Time" (RFC 3339 subset).
//                      Carries the VERBATIM lexeme — the
//                      lexeme-preserving decision applied to time: v4
//                      has no temporal substrate, so collapsing to a
//                      structured instant would fabricate a
//                      calendar/offset interpretation and lose
//                      round-trip (B-6) exactness. The four datetime
//                      sub-kinds are a tag-resolution distinction the
//                      deferred parser draws over the lexeme; the
//                      temporal interpretation is a deferred read
//                      (🟡 below).
//   - TomlArray     — §Array. Ordered, heterogeneous; carrier is the
//                      kernel `List<TomlValue>` (order is the list
//                      order — single authority, canonical per
//                      CULTURE §8).
//   - TomlInlineTable — §"Inline Table". A key/value table written
//                      inline (`{ … }`); TOML 1.0 §"Inline Table":
//                      inline tables are SELF-CONTAINED — keys/sub-
//                      tables cannot be added after definition. Carrier
//                      `Map<String, TomlValue>`.
//   - TomlTable     — §Table (`[table]` / dotted). A standard table
//                      that, per TOML 1.0 §Table, MAY be assembled
//                      across the document until closed — a distinct
//                      construct from the immutable inline table (the
//                      mutability/provenance distinction TOML 1.0 draws;
//                      see ledger pattern 2). Carrier `Map<String,
//                      TomlValue>` (key non-emptiness/charset is the
//                      deferred §Keys refinement, 🟡 below).
//
// Recursion lives in the `elements` / `entries` children (node.dag A1:
// the one recursive type is `Node`; this recursive `Disj` is a finite
// Node tree whose recursive occurrence is the name-reference `TomlValue`
// resolved through the namespace — the std/nat.dag `Succ { prev: Nat }`
// shape). REGULAR recursion ⇒ unconditionally admissible per A1;
// structural descent on a proper sub-TomlValue is the A2 IMPLICIT
// termination case, total by construction, no ranking measure.
//
// CLOSED. The eight are TOML v1.0.0's exhaustive value space — no
// `Other` / open variant. A ninth kind is an edit to this declaration
// routed to ratification (it would mean TOML changed), not a silent
// escape.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the value kind is consumed UNIFORMLY by
//      every TOML consumer (the deferred parse builds it, emit walks
//      it, any ingest dispatches on it). Shared classifier of the whole
//      TOML value space, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the eight carry structurally DISTINCT
//      payloads (nullary-free: a `String`; int `lexeme`; float
//      `lexeme`; `Bool`; datetime `lexeme`; `List<TomlValue>`; two
//      `Map<String, TomlValue>`). Collapsing to a flat `{ kind, … }`
//      record makes illegal mixed states representable — the inverse of
//      INVARIANTS P2. The two table variants are NOT a spurious
//      flag-collapse despite a shared payload SHAPE: TOML v1.0.0 draws
//      a real construct distinction — an inline table is self-contained
//      and immutable once defined (§"Inline Table"), a standard table
//      may be assembled across the document (§Table). Folding them to
//      one variant + a `bool inline` flag erases that mutability/
//      provenance fact and ratifies the very kind of stringly/flag
//      proxy M4 rejects; the two distinct constructors are the faithful
//      form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: a TOML value is TOML
//      v1.0.0's value space; heterogeneous external data, not the
//      carrier of a richer std/algebra.dag structure (no Monoid/Ring it
//      inhabits). Array/table children ground in their OWN carriers
//      (List = FreeMonoid, Map) owned by std/collection.dag — not a
//      richer single source TomlValue decomposes into; pointing the
//      variants at an algebra is circular.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a TOML value is EXACTLY ONE of the eight
//      kinds; mutually-exclusive alternatives (a value is a string or
//      an integer or …, never several at once), never simultaneous
//      coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the eight variants are
//      structurally heterogeneous (a `String`; int `lexeme`; float
//      `lexeme`; `Bool`; datetime `lexeme`; `List<TomlValue>`; two
//      distinct-construct `Map<String, TomlValue>`) — NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared enumerable set
//      (Practice 7). They are the irreducible constructors of the TOML
//      v1.0.0 value space, not a mechanical copy of an enumerable set.
// Terminal: the irreducible closed TOML v1.0.0 value space; a recursive
// Disj whose Array/InlineTable/Table children recurse through kernel
// `List`/`Map` of `TomlValue` (A1 recursion-in-children, name-reference
// through the namespace — the std/nat.dag `Nat` shape).
type TomlValue
  = TomlString { value: String }
  | TomlInt { lexeme: String }
  | TomlFloat { lexeme: String }
  | TomlBool { value: Bool }
  | TomlDatetime { lexeme: String }
  | TomlArray { elements: List<TomlValue> }
  | TomlInlineTable { entries: Map<String, TomlValue> }
  | TomlTable { entries: Map<String, TomlValue> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Deferred — TRACKED SCAFFOLD (🟡). The operations + refinements ride
// substrate that is scaffold today. Per the operator-ratified
// declarative-now / operations-deferred-with-owner discipline (verified
// on std/collection.dag #3169 + canonical json.dag #3184) these are NOT
// authored here — not improvised, not bodiless `fn` (the frozen v2 seed
// rejects bodiless `fn`, DECISIONS PARSE-1). Each carries the three
// bridge properties (scaffold doc / bounds on use / dissolution
// trigger).
//
// (1) `toml_parse` / `toml_emit` — the parse and emit operation bodies.
//   A TOML parser is a Char-STREAM structural walk over the TOML v1.0.0
//   grammar and emit builds a String from structure; both require
//   std/text.dag's `Char` + a `String` ↔ sequence-of-`Char`
//   decomposition — std/text.dag is T-3 SCAFFOLD. RATIFIED parse
//   strategy (mirrors json.dag): RECURSIVE-DESCENT single self-recursive
//   structural walk over the input Char-sequence spine; termination is
//   the A2 IMPLICIT case (descent on a proper sub-sequence).
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; `toml_parse`/`toml_emit` are
//         intentionally absent from toml.dag today.
//     (b) bounds on use — no consumer reads `toml_parse`/`toml_emit`
//         from toml.dag in this PR. The carrier seam is fixed: when
//         authored, `toml_parse : String -> Outcome<TomlValue>` and
//         `toml_emit : TomlValue -> String`, `Outcome<T>` the ratified
//         D1 carrier — fail-closed (every non-conforming input,
//         duplicate key, invalid datetime is `Rejected { diagnostic }`,
//         never a partial/defaulted value). The SAME seam as the other
//         five T-4.6 formats.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/text.dag (T-3) lands its
//         `Char` + `String` ↔ `List<Char>` decomposition, a follow-up
//         PR adds `toml_parse`/`toml_emit` returning `Outcome<TomlValue>`;
//         this note dissolves. The T-4 directive's "operations
//         deferred-with-named-owner" + TASKS.md T-4.6 record the same
//         dependency.
//
// (2) Numeric interpretation of `TomlInt.lexeme` / `TomlFloat.lexeme` —
//   the integer tower (std/integer.dag) + IEEE-754 `Float`
//   (std/float.dag) are scaffold (T-3).
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the lexeme is not a numeric value.
//     (b) bounds on use — a consumer needing the numeric value waits on
//         the interpretation read; the lexeme is round-trip-exact (B-6).
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/integer.dag + std/float.dag
//         land, a follow-up adds `toml_int_value`/`toml_float_value :
//         … -> Outcome<…>`; this note dissolves.
//
// (3) Temporal interpretation of `TomlDatetime.lexeme` — TOML v1.0.0
//   §Date-Time is an RFC 3339 subset with four sub-kinds (offset
//   date-time / local date-time / local date / local time). v4 has NO
//   temporal substrate; collapsing the lexeme to a structured instant
//   would fabricate a calendar/offset interpretation.
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; `TomlDatetime` carries the verbatim
//         RFC 3339 lexeme; no temporal interpretation is claimed.
//     (b) bounds on use — a consumer needing the instant / sub-kind
//         waits on the temporal substrate; the lexeme round-trips (B-6)
//         exactly and the deferred parser validates RFC-3339-subset
//         conformance fail-closed.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when a v4 temporal substrate lands
//         (no owner today — its absence is itself surfaced to T-4 mgr;
//         a new std/ temporal concept is a substrate-extension STOP,
//         not a worker decision), a follow-up adds
//         `toml_datetime_value : TomlDatetime -> Outcome<…>` over the
//         four sub-kinds; this note dissolves.
//
// (4) Non-empty / charset key refinement on `TomlTable` /
//   `TomlInlineTable` — the immutable scaffold header's
//   `Map<NonEmptyStr, TomlValue>`. v4 has no `NonEmptyStr` (a String
//   refinement; std/text.dag refinement substrate is T-3 scaffold).
//   The carrier here is kernel `Map<String, TomlValue>`; TOML §Keys
//   conformance (bare-key charset; quoted keys — incl. the spec-
//   permitted EMPTY quoted key, so the refined type must NOT be a
//   blanket non-empty) is enforced fail-closed by the deferred parser.
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note + the header reconciliation block;
//         the `String` key is unrefined here by design.
//     (b) bounds on use — no consumer relies on a key-refinement type
//         from toml.dag in this PR; the parser is the §Keys authority
//         meanwhile.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/text.dag's String-refinement
//         substrate lands, a follow-up refines the key type to a
//         TOML-§Keys-faithful refinement (bare/quoted rules, NOT a
//         blanket non-empty — the spec permits empty quoted keys);
//         this note dissolves. The header/spec strictness discrepancy
//         is flagged to T-4 mgr (surfaced, not silently encoded).

